+    <section class="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-heading">
+      <p class="journal-kicker">From origin to roast</p>
+      <h2 id="journal-heading">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <p class="journal-intro">Good coffee begins long before the first crack. Here’s how we choose the beans that reach your cup.</p>
+
+      <div class="journal-story" id="journal-story">
+        <p>Our sourcing process starts with people and place. Before we choose a coffee, we want to understand who grew it, where it was harvested, and how it travelled from the farm to our roastery. Altitude, soil, shade, and rainfall all shape a bean’s character, but so do the decisions a grower makes throughout the season. We look for partners who can tell that whole story, including the challenges behind a harvest, rather than simply sending us a tasting score.</p>
+        <p>We work with growers, cooperatives, and importers who help us trace coffees back to their origins. Together, we discuss harvest timing, processing methods, and the care taken during drying and storage. We ask how prices are agreed and how the people producing the coffee are paid. Building lasting relationships matters to us because a dependable buyer gives producers more room to plan for the next season, invest in their farms, and keep improving quality. A great lot is an invitation to begin a relationship, not just complete a purchase.</p>
+        <p>Every potential coffee then comes to the cupping table. We roast small samples and taste them side by side, looking for sweetness, balance, clarity, and a finish that makes us want another sip. We taste without milk or sugar first, then consider how each coffee will work in the drinks you enjoy at home or in our café. A bright, fruit-forward lot might shine as a filter brew, while a rounder coffee with cocoa notes may become the heart of an espresso. We repeat these tastings before making a selection, because consistency matters as much as an exciting first impression.</p>
+        <p>Care for the land is part of those conversations, too. We ask our sourcing partners about soil health, water use during processing, shade trees, and the practical steps farms take to protect their local environment. There is no single approach that suits every growing region, so we value specific practices and honest answers. We also pay attention to how coffee is packed and transported, choosing protective storage that helps preserve quality and planning purchases around harvest cycles rather than treating beans as an endlessly interchangeable ingredient.</p>
+        <p>When a shipment arrives, we compare it with the samples we approved and develop a roast profile in small batches. Our aim is to bring out the character already present in the bean, whether that means a gentle floral aroma, caramel sweetness, or a rich chocolate finish. We keep tasting as the coffee rests and as we brew it, adjusting our approach when needed. From the first conversation at origin to the final roast, sourcing is an ongoing practice of listening, tasting, and learning. That care is what we hope you notice in every cup.</p>
+      </div>
